1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:08,764
On the morning of January 7th, 2009, the chairman of one of India's biggest companies sat down and wrote a letter.

2
00:00:09,114 --> 00:00:12,687
In it, he admitted something nobody believed was possible.

3
00:00:13,387 --> 00:00:16,988
The billions of rupees his company said it had in the bank?

4
00:00:17,488 --> 00:00:19,392
Almost all of it… didn't exist.

5
00:00:20,342 --> 00:00:24,351
By evening, the company's share price had crashed nearly 80 percent.

6
00:00:24,701 --> 00:00:28,626
Fifty thousand employees didn't know if they still had jobs.

7
00:00:28,976 --> 00:00:35,346
And India's entire IT industry, the pride of the nation, was suddenly under suspicion.

8
00:00:36,296 --> 00:00:39,550
The strangest part? The company's name was Satyam.

9
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:41,847
In Sanskrit, Satyam means…

10
00:00:42,347 --> 00:00:42,967
Truth.

11
00:00:51,617 --> 00:00:55,607
The story starts in Hyderabad in 1987.

12
00:00:55,957 --> 00:01:06,239
A businessman named B. Ramalinga Raju, from a family that had worked in textiles and real estate, starts a small software company with his brother.

13
00:01:06,589 --> 00:01:09,057
They call it Satyam Computer Services.

14
00:01:10,007 --> 00:01:11,377
The timing is perfect.

15
00:01:11,877 --> 00:01:16,396
The world is going digital, and Western companies have discovered something:

16
00:01:16,746 --> 00:01:20,931
Indian engineers can write the same code for a fraction of the cost.

17
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:23,795
Then comes the Y2K panic.

18
00:01:24,145 --> 00:01:29,189
Every company on earth needs old software fixed before the year 2000,

19
00:01:29,539 --> 00:01:32,947
and Indian IT firms are flooded with work.

20
00:01:33,897 --> 00:01:35,663
Satyam grows explosively.

21
00:01:36,013 --> 00:01:39,951
In 2001, it lists on the New York Stock Exchange.

22
00:01:40,301 --> 00:01:51,403
By 2008 it has over 50,000 employees, offices around the world, and clients that include a huge list of Fortune 500 companies.

23
00:01:52,353 --> 00:01:58,931
It's one of the "Big Four" of Indian IT, alongside TCS, Infosys and Wipro.

24
00:01:59,881 --> 00:02:03,532
And in 2008, Satyam wins a prestigious award…

25
00:02:03,832 --> 00:02:05,938
for excellence in corporate governance.

26
00:02:06,538 --> 00:02:08,336
In other words, for being honest.

27
00:02:09,036 --> 00:02:11,380
Remember that trophy. We'll come back to it.

28
00:02:15,330 --> 00:02:17,148
Here's what nobody outside knew.

29
00:02:17,748 --> 00:02:24,127
Years earlier, Satyam's real profits had started falling a little short of what the market expected.

30
00:02:24,477 --> 00:02:30,019
The gap was small. A few numbers adjusted. Just enough to keep the share price happy.

31
00:02:30,369 --> 00:02:36,230
The plan, presumably, was simple: next year would be better, and the gap would quietly close.

32
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:38,446
But it never closed.

33
00:02:39,046 --> 00:02:44,539
Every year, the fake profit had to match the fake profit from the year before, plus growth.

34
00:02:44,889 --> 00:02:47,453
Every lie needed a bigger lie to cover it.

35
00:02:48,403 --> 00:02:54,168
And fake profits create a problem: if you claim you earned money, that money has to be somewhere.

36
00:02:54,518 --> 00:02:56,267
So the money appeared on paper.

37
00:02:56,767 --> 00:03:01,580
Cash balances. Bank deposits. Interest earned on those deposits.

38
00:03:01,930 --> 00:03:12,304
According to investigators, thousands of fake invoices were created for work that was never done, and bank documents were forged to show money that was never there.

39
00:03:13,254 --> 00:03:16,426
On paper, Satyam was sitting on a mountain of cash.

40
00:03:17,026 --> 00:03:19,701
In reality, the mountain was made of paper.

41
00:03:23,651 --> 00:03:30,388
By 2008, the hole had grown so big it couldn't be hidden much longer. Raju needed a way out.

42
00:03:30,738 --> 00:03:33,960
And it seems he found one in a company called Maytas.

43
00:03:34,310 --> 00:03:35,292
Notice something?

44
00:03:35,892 --> 00:03:38,304
Maytas is Satyam… spelled backwards.

45
00:03:39,254 --> 00:03:46,885
Maytas Properties and Maytas Infra were real-estate and infrastructure companies controlled by Raju's family.

46
00:03:47,235 --> 00:03:54,802
On December 16th, 2008, Satyam announced it would buy them for about 1.6 billion dollars.

47
00:03:55,152 --> 00:04:01,144
On the surface, it made no sense. Why would a software company buy construction businesses?

48
00:04:02,094 --> 00:04:03,830
But look at it from the inside.

49
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:10,702
If Satyam "spent" its fake cash buying real companies, the fake money would disappear into a deal…

50
00:04:11,052 --> 00:04:13,440
and be replaced by real buildings and land.

51
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:15,891
The hole would be filled. Forever.

52
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:19,747
It almost worked. But investors were furious.

53
00:04:20,097 --> 00:04:25,086
Why was the company spending shareholders' money on the founder's family businesses?

54
00:04:25,436 --> 00:04:29,584
Satyam's shares crashed. Within about a day, the deal was cancelled.

55
00:04:30,534 --> 00:04:31,903
Then the hits kept coming.

56
00:04:32,253 --> 00:04:37,215
Days later, it emerged that the World Bank had banned Satyam from its contracts.

57
00:04:37,565 --> 00:04:39,962
Independent directors began resigning.

58
00:04:40,312 --> 00:04:42,098
The escape door had slammed shut.

59
00:04:42,598 --> 00:04:44,992
And Raju was trapped inside his own lie.

60
00:04:48,942 --> 00:04:52,447
Which brings us back to January 7th, 2009.

61
00:04:52,797 --> 00:04:59,711
Raju's letter to the board was addressed to the directors and the stock exchanges. And it was a full confession.

62
00:05:00,661 --> 00:05:06,141
The cash and bank balances: overstated by more than 5,000 crore rupees.

63
00:05:06,491 --> 00:05:10,018
That's over a billion dollars that simply did not exist.

64
00:05:10,368 --> 00:05:13,219
Interest on deposits that didn't exist: fake.

65
00:05:13,569 --> 00:05:16,018
Money owed by customers: inflated.

66
00:05:16,368 --> 00:05:22,297
Even the latest quarter: the company claimed operating margins of around 24 percent.

67
00:05:22,647 --> 00:05:24,895
The real figure was about 3 percent.

68
00:05:25,845 --> 00:05:31,186
In the letter, Raju described his situation with a line that would become famous:

69
00:05:31,536 --> 00:05:36,119
"It was like riding a tiger, not knowing how to get off without being eaten."

70
00:05:36,819 --> 00:05:44,940
He also claimed he hadn't taken a single rupee for himself from the inflated numbers. Investigators would later dispute that.

71
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:53,516
That day, Satyam's shares lost almost 80 percent of their value. Billions in investor money vanished in hours.

72
00:05:53,866 --> 00:05:55,761
And remember that governance trophy?

73
00:05:56,261 --> 00:05:57,353
It was taken back.

74
00:06:01,303 --> 00:06:04,415
Two days later, Ramalinga Raju was arrested.

75
00:06:04,765 --> 00:06:07,555
The Indian government did something unusual.

76
00:06:07,905 --> 00:06:12,971
It dissolved Satyam's board and appointed its own directors to save the company.

77
00:06:13,321 --> 00:06:20,012
Not to save Raju, but to save the tens of thousands of employees and the clients depending on them.

78
00:06:20,962 --> 00:06:25,436
In 2009, Tech Mahindra won the bid to take over Satyam.

79
00:06:25,786 --> 00:06:32,124
The company survived, renamed Mahindra Satyam, and was later merged fully into Tech Mahindra.

80
00:06:32,474 --> 00:06:34,110
The name "Satyam" disappeared.

81
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:37,821
The court case dragged on for six years.

82
00:06:38,171 --> 00:06:47,115
In 2015, a special court convicted Raju, his brother and several others, sentencing them to seven years in prison.

83
00:06:47,465 --> 00:06:48,510
And the auditors?

84
00:06:49,010 --> 00:06:55,707
Satyam's accounts had been audited by PricewaterhouseCoopers, one of the biggest audit firms in the world.

85
00:06:56,057 --> 00:07:07,797
Its auditors were also convicted in India, and US regulators fined PwC's Indian affiliates millions of dollars for failing to catch the fraud.

86
00:07:11,347 --> 00:07:14,051
So how did a lie this big survive for years?

87
00:07:14,651 --> 00:07:19,283
One: auditors trusted documents instead of checking with the banks directly.

88
00:07:19,633 --> 00:07:25,632
Two: the board, including well-known independent directors, approved what the founder wanted.

89
00:07:25,982 --> 00:07:33,026
Three: everyone was making money. When a share price keeps going up, very few people ask hard questions.

90
00:07:33,976 --> 00:07:36,446
Satyam's fall changed Indian business.

91
00:07:36,796 --> 00:07:45,440
Corporate governance rules were tightened, auditors faced new scrutiny, and "Satyam" became shorthand for corporate fraud in India.

92
00:07:46,790 --> 00:07:52,544
A company named after truth, built on one of the biggest lies in Indian business history.

93
00:07:53,144 --> 00:07:57,437
It started as a small gap between real numbers and reported ones.

94
00:07:57,787 --> 00:08:01,769
And that's the lesson: frauds this big almost never begin big.

95
00:08:02,119 --> 00:08:05,959
They begin small… and then they become a tiger you can't get off.

96
00:08:07,509 --> 00:08:14,836
Next time on Paper Empires: how the world's biggest phone company went from number one to nothing in six years.

97
00:08:15,186 --> 00:08:17,000
Subscribe, and I'll see you there.
